<h2>What a frenectomy treats</h2>
<p>A <strong>frenum</strong> is the small fold of tissue that connects your tongue to the floor of your mouth, or your lip to your gums. When it's too tight, too thick, or attached too close to the teeth, it can limit movement and cause real problems — a condition commonly called a <strong>tongue-tie</strong> (ankyloglossia) or <strong>lip-tie</strong>. A frenectomy gently releases that tissue so everything can move freely.</p>

<h2>Signs a frenectomy may help</h2>
<ul class="checklist">
  <li><strong>Infants:</strong> difficulty latching or nursing, clicking sounds while feeding, or slow weight gain</li>
  <li><strong>Children:</strong> speech difficulties, trouble eating certain foods, or a pronounced gap between the front teeth</li>
  <li><strong>Adults:</strong> gum recession pulled on by a tight frenum, orthodontic relapse, or dentures that won't seat comfortably</li>
</ul>

<h2>What to expect</h2>
<ul class="checklist">
  <li><strong>Exam &amp; recommendation.</strong> We confirm the frenum is the cause and walk you through whether a release is warranted — no pressure.</li>
  <li><strong>The procedure.</strong> The release is done under local anesthesia and usually takes only a few minutes.</li>
  <li><strong>Aftercare.</strong> We'll give you simple stretches or exercises to prevent reattachment while the area heals over the next week or two.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Comfortable, honest care in Tempe</h2>
<p>Dr. Phillips performs frenectomies for patients across Tempe, Mesa, Chandler, Gilbert, and the greater Phoenix East Valley. If a frenectomy isn't the right answer for your situation, we'll tell you — and point you toward what is. Sedation options are available for anxious patients or longer combined visits.</p>

